textosLlegan("theory", {"hf-kant-poetas":{"html":"<p>Between 1770 and 1805, in a handful of small German towns (Königsberg, Weimar, Jena), poets and philosophers read one another, wrote to one another, criticised one another and sometimes became friends. Kant hardly ever left the area around Königsberg and never met Goethe or Schiller, but his books changed what they wrote. Hamann and Herder, who did know him, were his first opponents. And the young people of Jena (the Schlegel brothers, Novalis, Hölderlin, Schelling, Hegel) grew up reading Kant, Fichte and Goethe all at once. This exploration tells the story of that conversation. In literature it covers three stages: <strong>Sturm und Drang</strong>, <strong>Weimar Classicism</strong> and <strong>early Romanticism</strong>. None of them can be understood without the philosophy of its time, and the philosophy of Kant and his successors cannot be understood without the poets either.</p>\n<p>A detail to begin with: the name of the subject <strong>World Literature</strong> comes from one of the protagonists. In 1827 Goethe told his secretary Eckermann that national literature no longer meant very much and that the age of <em>Weltliteratur</em>, ‘world literature’, had arrived.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: who do you think has more influence on how we understand the world, a philosopher or a novelist? Can a novel change an idea, or does it only illustrate it? Keep your answer and come back to it at the end.</p>\n\n<div class=\"figwrap\"><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/weimar_musenhof.jpg\" alt=\"The Weimar ‘Musenhof’ (Court of the Muses): Schiller reads in Tiefurt Park before Duchess Anna Amalia, Goethe, Herder and Wieland. Idealised scene by Theobald von Oer (1860)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The Weimar ‘Musenhof’ (Court of the Muses): Schiller reads in Tiefurt Park before Duchess Anna Amalia, Goethe, Herder and Wieland. Idealised scene by Theobald von Oer (1860)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/kant_tischgenossen.jpg\" alt=\"Kant and his table companions (Emil Doerstling, 1892-1893). Kant dined with friends every day and kept the conversation going for hours; the scene was painted a century later\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Kant and his table companions (Emil Doerstling, 1892-1893). Kant dined with friends every day and kept the conversation going for hours; the scene was painted a century later</figcaption></figure></div>\n<h2>1. The map: three towns and a conversation</h2>\n<p>Germany was not a country but more than three hundred states. There was no cultural capital like Paris or London. Intellectual life was spread across small courts and universities, and that is why a few people in a few towns were able to have so much contact with one another. <strong>Königsberg</strong>, in East Prussia, was the city of Kant and Hamann. <strong>Weimar</strong>, capital of a tiny duchy, drew Goethe (1775), Herder (1776) and Schiller (1799). <strong>Jena</strong>, some twenty kilometres away, had the duchy’s university: Schiller, Fichte, Schelling and Hegel taught there, and the early Romantics gathered there.</p>\n<div class=\"figwrap\"><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/kant_casa.jpg\" alt=\"Königsberg Castle with Kant’s house on the left (old engraving)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Königsberg Castle with Kant’s house on the left (old engraving)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/konigsberg_castillo.jpg\" alt=\"Königsberg Castle around 1900. The city was razed in 1944-1945; today it is Kaliningrad (Russia)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Königsberg Castle around 1900. The city was razed in 1944-1945; today it is Kaliningrad (Russia)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/weimar_casa_goethe.jpg\" alt=\"Goethe’s Garden House in the Park on the Ilm, in Weimar, his first home in the town (1776-1782). Photochrom, around 1900\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Goethe’s Garden House in the Park on the Ilm, in Weimar, his first home in the town (1776-1782). Photochrom, around 1900</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/weimar_casa_schiller.jpg\" alt=\"Schiller’s House in Weimar, where he lived from 1802 until his death in 1805. Photochrom, around 1900\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Schiller’s House in Weimar, where he lived from 1802 until his death in 1805. Photochrom, around 1900</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/jena_1900.jpg\" alt=\"Jena around 1900. In the 1790s its university brought together Schiller, Fichte, Schelling, Hegel and the early Romantics\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Jena around 1900. In the 1790s its university brought together Schiller, Fichte, Schelling, Hegel and the early Romantics</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/tubinga_1900.jpg\" alt=\"Tübingen and the Neckar (1894). Hegel, Hölderlin and Schelling shared a room at its Protestant seminary\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Tübingen and the Neckar (1894). Hegel, Hölderlin and Schelling shared a room at its Protestant seminary</figcaption></figure></div>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Life</th><th>Role</th><th>Relationship with Kant</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Immanuel Kant</strong></td><td>1724-1804</td><td>Philosopher; hardly ever left Königsberg</td><td>He is the centre of the conversation (Topics 19 and 20)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Johann Georg Hamann</strong></td><td>1730-1788</td><td>Religious thinker, the ‘Magus of the North’</td><td>Kant’s friend and neighbour, and his sharpest critic</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Johann Gottfried Herder</strong></td><td>1744-1803</td><td>Philosopher of language and of history</td><td>He was Kant’s student (1762-1764) and ended up as his opponent</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Johann Wolfgang von Goethe</strong></td><td>1749-1832</td><td>Poet, novelist, naturalist and minister in Weimar</td><td>He read the <em>Critique of Judgement</em> with enthusiasm, but distrusted abstract philosophy</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Friedrich Schiller</strong></td><td>1759-1805</td><td>Playwright, poet, historian</td><td>He studied Kant for years and argued with his ethics</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Johann Gottlieb Fichte</strong></td><td>1762-1814</td><td>Philosopher; professor at Jena (1794-1799)</td><td>He presented himself as Kant’s continuator; idealism starts from him</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Friedrich Schlegel</strong> and <strong>Novalis</strong></td><td>1772-1829 and 1772-1801</td><td>Critics and poets of early Romanticism</td><td>They come to Kant through Fichte</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Friedrich Hölderlin</strong></td><td>1770-1843</td><td>Poet</td><td>Fellow student of Hegel and Schelling at the Tübingen seminary</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n<div class=\"figwrap\"><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/retrato_kant.jpg\" alt=\"Immanuel Kant (Gottlieb Doebler, 1791)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Immanuel Kant (Gottlieb Doebler, 1791)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/retrato_hamann.jpg\" alt=\"Johann Georg Hamann\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Johann Georg Hamann</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/retrato_herder.jpg\" alt=\"Johann Gottfried Herder (Anton Graff, 1785)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Johann Gottfried Herder (Anton Graff, 1785)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/retrato_goethe_stieler.jpg\" alt=\"Johann Wolfgang von Goethe (Joseph Karl Stieler, 1828)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Johann Wolfgang von Goethe (Joseph Karl Stieler, 1828)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/retrato_schiller_graff.jpg\" alt=\"Friedrich Schiller (Anton Graff, 1786-1791)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Friedrich Schiller (Anton Graff, 1786-1791)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/retrato_fichte.jpg\" alt=\"Johann Gottlieb Fichte (engraving by Albrecht Schultheiss after Friedrich Bury)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Johann Gottlieb Fichte (engraving by Albrecht Schultheiss after Friedrich Bury)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/retrato_friedrich_schlegel.jpg\" alt=\"Friedrich Schlegel (Franz Gareis, 1801)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Friedrich Schlegel (Franz Gareis, 1801)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/retrato_novalis.jpg\" alt=\"Novalis (Franz Gareis, around 1799)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Novalis (Franz Gareis, around 1799)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/retrato_caroline.jpg\" alt=\"Caroline Schlegel, later Caroline Schelling (Johann Friedrich August Tischbein, 1798)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Caroline Schlegel, later Caroline Schelling (Johann Friedrich August Tischbein, 1798)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/retrato_holderlin.jpg\" alt=\"Friedrich Hölderlin (Franz Karl Hiemer, pastel, 1792)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Friedrich Hölderlin (Franz Karl Hiemer, pastel, 1792)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/retrato_hegel.jpg\" alt=\"Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel (Jakob Schlesinger, 1831)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel (Jakob Schlesinger, 1831)</figcaption></figure></div>\n<h2>2. Sturm und Drang: feeling against Enlightenment reason (c. 1770-1785)</h2>\n<p><em>Sturm und Drang</em> means ‘storm and stress’ (literally ‘storm and urge’). The name comes from a play by Klinger (1776) and is applied to a group of young writers who, in the 1770s, rebelled against Enlightenment taste: the rules of French theatre, correct poetry and the reason that measures everything. Against all this they championed <strong>feeling</strong>, <strong>nature</strong>, the <strong>freedom</strong> of the individual and the <strong>genius</strong>, the creator who does not follow rules because he invents them. Their model was Shakespeare, not Racine.</p>\n<p>The movement had two philosophical fathers, and both came from Kant’s circle.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hamann: there is no reason without language.</strong> Hamann lived in Königsberg and was a friend of Kant. In 1759 Kant and another friend tried to bring him back to the Enlightenment faith, and Hamann replied with a little book, <em>Socratic Memorabilia</em>, in which he uses Socrates against the Enlightenment: the wise man who knows that he does not know trusts his inner ‘genius’ more than reason. He summed up his central thesis in a letter to Herder (1784): ‘Reason is language, <em>lógos</em>’. There is no pure reason that can be separated from the words, the tradition and the history in which we think. That is why the ‘pure reason’ of the <em>Critique</em> (1781) seemed to him a mirage, and he wrote against it a <em>Metacritique on the Purism of Reason</em> (1784), which was not published until after his death.</li>\n<li><strong>Herder: each people, its own language and its own history.</strong> Herder was a student of Kant, who thought highly of him. His <em>Treatise on the Origin of Language</em> (1772) argues that language is neither a divine gift nor a useful invention, but something that arises from human nature itself. Later, in the <em>Ideas for the Philosophy of the History of Mankind</em> (1784-1791), he argues that every people and every age has its own value and its own way of being human: they are not steps on the way to enlightened Europe. Kant reviewed the <em>Ideas</em> in 1785 and criticised them for using imagination where concepts were needed. Herder did not forget it. In 1799 he published a <em>Metacritique</em> against the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em>, and in 1800, <em>Kalligone</em>, against Kant’s aesthetics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The decisive encounter for literature took place in Strasbourg, in the winter of 1770-1771. Herder, suffering from an eye complaint, spent a few months there. A twenty-one-year-old law student, Goethe, visited him every day. Herder opened his eyes to Shakespeare, to Homer, to the Bible read as poetry and to folk songs. From this came <em>Götz von Berlichingen</em> (1773), the poem <em>Prometheus</em> (1774) and, above all, <em>The Sorrows of Young Werther</em> (1774). Werther is a novel in letters about a young man who feels so intensely that there is no room for him in the world. It was read all over Europe, people copied the clothes of its hero and suicides were attributed to it. Seven years later Schiller premiered <em>The Robbers</em> (1781): a nobleman’s son, betrayed by his brother, puts himself at the head of a band of outlaws to rebel against an unjust world.</p>\n<div class=\"figwrap\"><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/estrasburgo_catedral.jpg\" alt=\"Strasbourg around 1895. In the background, the cathedral that inspired Goethe’s essay ‘On German Architecture’ (1772). Photochrom by Photoglob Zürich\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Strasbourg around 1895. In the background, the cathedral that inspired Goethe’s essay ‘On German Architecture’ (1772). Photochrom by Photoglob Zürich</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/libro_werther_1774.jpg\" alt=\"Title page of the first edition of Werther (Leipzig, 1774)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Title page of the first edition of <em>Werther</em> (Leipzig, 1774)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/werther_chodowiecki.jpg\" alt=\"Werther embraces Lotte (Daniel Chodowiecki, 1775). The novel was illustrated and imitated all over Europe\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Werther embraces Lotte (Daniel Chodowiecki, 1775). The novel was illustrated and imitated all over Europe</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/libro_raeuber_1781.jpg\" alt=\"First edition of The Robbers (1781), published without the author’s name. Wikipedia: Photo H.-P. Haack\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>First edition of <em>The Robbers</em> (1781), published without the author’s name. Wikipedia: Photo H.-P. Haack</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/schiller_bopser.jpg\" alt=\"Schiller reads The Robbers to his friends in the Bopser woods, in Stuttgart (sketch by Viktor Heideloff, a fellow student of his)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Schiller reads <em>The Robbers</em> to his friends in the Bopser woods, in Stuttgart (sketch by Viktor Heideloff, a fellow student of his)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/fussli_pesadilla.jpg\" alt=\"The Nightmare (Henry Fuseli, 1781), from the same year as The Robbers: the dark and the irrational enter art\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption><em>The Nightmare</em> (Henry Fuseli, 1781), from the same year as <em>The Robbers</em>: the dark and the irrational enter art</figcaption></figure></div>\n<p>There is an episode that shows just how much literature and philosophy were intertwined. Jacobi recounted that he had shown Lessing Goethe's <em>Prometheus</em>, still unpublished, and that Lessing, on reading it, had declared himself a follower of Spinoza: ‘Hen kai pan’, ‘one and all’. In 1785, Jacobi made it public. The <strong>pantheism controversy</strong> broke out, with Mendelssohn, Herder and Kant himself taking part, and the question was whether reason inevitably leads to Spinozism, that is, to a God who merges with nature. A poem had set off one of the great philosophical debates of the century.</p>\n<p>How Spinoza, despised for a century, became a point of reference for Goethe, Herder and the Romantics, and later for Hegel, Nietzsche, Einstein and Deleuze, is told in the exploration <a href=\"#teoria/hf-spinoza-universal\">‘Universal Spinoza’</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Enlightenment (Kant, Mendelssohn)</th><th>Sturm und Drang (Hamann, Herder, the young Goethe)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Decisive faculty</td><td>Reason, the same in everyone</td><td>Feeling, intuition, faith</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Language</td><td>A tool for expressing ideas</td><td>The very form of thought: ‘reason is language’</td></tr>\n<tr><td>History</td><td>Progress towards humanity's maturity</td><td>A plurality of peoples and eras, each with its own value</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Art</td><td>Subject to rules and good taste</td><td>The work of genius, which creates its own rules</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Model</td><td>The French classics</td><td>Shakespeare, folk poetry, the Bible</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Think about it: Hamann says there is no reason without language. If that is true, can someone who thinks in Basque and someone who thinks in Spanish reach exactly the same conclusions? What, then, would the ‘universal’ truth the Enlightenment speaks of be?</p>\n\n<h2>3. Kant in the middle: the <em>Critique of Judgement</em> (1790)</h2>\n<p>In the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (1781), Kant studies what we can know (Topic 19). In the <em>Critique of Practical Reason</em> (1788), what we ought to do (Topic 20). He still lacks a bridge between the two worlds, nature governed by laws and freedom. He looks for it in the third critique, the <strong>Critique of Judgement</strong> (1790), which deals with the beautiful, the sublime and living beings. It is the book of Kant’s that artists read most, because of four ideas:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The beautiful pleases without interest.</strong> When I say that a flower is beautiful I do not want it for anything: neither to eat it nor to sell it. Aesthetic pleasure is disinterested, and that is why I can demand that others share it even though I cannot prove it to them with concepts.</li>\n<li><strong>Beauty is ‘purposiveness without purpose’.</strong> A beautiful work seems to have been made on purpose, perfectly fitted together, yet it serves no purpose. In it, imagination and understanding play freely, without either ruling over the other.</li>\n<li><strong>The sublime.</strong> Faced with a storm at sea or an immense mountain range, we feel small, and at the same time we discover in ourselves something greater than any nature: reason and freedom. The Romantics made the sublime one of their great themes.</li>\n<li><strong>Genius.</strong> Kant defines genius as ‘the talent (natural gift) that gives the rule to art’. This seems to prove Sturm und Drang right, but with a qualification: the genius cannot explain how he creates, and taste has to discipline him. Kant does not accept that genius stands above reason.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"figwrap\"><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/libro_kdu_1790.jpg\" alt=\"Title page of the first edition of the Critique of Judgement (Berlin and Libau, 1790)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Title page of the first edition of the <em>Critique of Judgement</em> (Berlin and Libau, 1790)</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/temas2/romanticismo_idealismo_Caspar_David_Friedrich_-_Wanderer_above_the_Sea_of_Fog.jpg\" alt=\"Wanderer above the Sea of Fog (Caspar David Friedrich, around 1818): the sublime in Romantic painting\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption><em>Wanderer above the Sea of Fog</em> (Caspar David Friedrich, around 1818): the sublime in Romantic painting</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/temas2/romanticismo_idealismo_Friedrich_Caspar_David_-_Mönch_am_Meer_-_Alte_Nationalgalerie_in_Berlin.jpg\" alt=\"The Monk by the Sea (Caspar David Friedrich, 1808-1810): the human being, tiny before the infinite\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption><em>The Monk by the Sea</em> (Caspar David Friedrich, 1808-1810): the human being, tiny before the infinite</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/friedrich_kreidefelsen.jpg\" alt=\"Chalk Cliffs on Rügen (Caspar David Friedrich, 1818)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption><em>Chalk Cliffs on Rügen</em> (Caspar David Friedrich, 1818)</figcaption></figure></div>\n<p>Goethe said that the <em>Critique of Judgement</em> gave him one of the happiest periods of his life, because in it he saw the work of art and the work of nature treated together. Schiller read it pencil in hand and built his whole aesthetics on it.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: Kant says that the beautiful pleases ‘without interest’. Is that true of the music you listen to, the clothes you wear or the photos you post? Have you ever had an experience of beauty that was truly disinterested?</p>\n\n<h2>4. Schiller against (and with) Kant: duty, inclination and beauty</h2>\n<p>Schiller is the clearest case of a poet who becomes a philosopher by reading Kant. Between 1791 and 1796 he wrote hardly any plays: he studied the three <em>Critiques</em> and wrote essays. He accepted the essentials of Kant: freedom, autonomy and human dignity. But he had one objection to his ethics.</p>\n<p>For Kant (Topic 20), an action only has moral worth if it is done <strong>from duty</strong>, not from inclination. Someone who helps another person because it comes from the heart acts in accordance with duty, but their action does not yet have moral worth: that worth appears when they help even though they do not feel like it, only because the moral law commands it. Schiller saw the risk in this idea and mocked it in two epigrams from the <em>Xenien</em> (1796), entitled ‘Scruple of Conscience’ and ‘Decision’:</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Gladly I serve my friends, but alas, I do it from inclination, / and so it often gnaws at me that I am not virtuous.</p>\n<p>There is no other way: you must try to despise them / and then do with loathing what duty commands you.</p></blockquote>\n<p>It is a caricature, and Schiller knew it. His serious proposal is in <strong><em>On Grace and Dignity</em></strong> (1793). There he defends the ideal of the <strong>beautiful soul</strong>: a person in whom reason and sensibility, duty and inclination, no longer fight, because they have educated their feelings until they desire what duty commands. <strong>Grace</strong> is the visible expression of that harmony. <strong>Dignity</strong> appears when harmony is not possible and inclination has to be overcome for the sake of duty, like the tragic hero.</p>\n<p>Kant replied. In the second edition of <em>Religion within the Boundaries of Mere Reason</em> (1794) he added a note. He praises Schiller’s essay, written ‘with a masterly hand’, and denies that his ethics imposes a ‘Carthusian’ frame of mind, gloomy and penitent. But he does not give way on the essentials: duty cannot depend on grace, because the moral law commands unconditionally and awakens respect, not affection. Even so, Kant admits that a cheerful heart in doing one’s duty is a sign that virtue is genuine. The two are closer than it seems: they are arguing about whether feeling can be part of moral life, not about whether duty exists.</p>\n<p>Schiller went even further in the <strong><em>Letters on the Aesthetic Education of Man</em></strong> (1795), written while France was living through its Revolution. His concern is a political question: how is it possible that an uprising undertaken in the name of freedom and reason ended in the Terror? In his view, because political freedom cannot be granted to those who have not received an education that prepares them to exercise it. Within each person two forces clash: a <strong>sensuous impulse</strong>, linked to matter, life and change, and a <strong>formal impulse</strong>, linked to law, reason and what endures. The reconciliation between the two is achieved only by a third, the <strong>play impulse</strong>, whose object is the beautiful. Hence the most famous sentence in the <em>Letters</em>: the human being 'is only fully human when he plays'. Thus, educating aesthetic sensibility becomes the path leading to political freedom.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Kant</th><th>Schiller</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Moral worth</td><td>Only in the action done from duty</td><td>Higher still when duty and inclination coincide</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Feelings</td><td>They cannot ground morality; at most, they accompany it</td><td>They can be educated until they desire the good</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ideal</td><td>The good will, which obeys the law it gives itself</td><td>The beautiful soul, in which reason and sensibility are in harmony</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Role of art</td><td>Symbol of morality; bridge between nature and freedom</td><td>The path of moral and political education</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What they share</td><td colspan=\"2\">Human freedom and dignity, and the rejection of a morality based on rewards or on happiness</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: two people help a classmate to study. One does it reluctantly, because they believe they ought to; the other, because they enjoy it. Which of the two acts better according to Kant? And according to Schiller? And according to you?</p>\n\n<h2>5. Goethe: the idea you can see</h2>\n<p>Goethe was never a Kantian. He distrusted abstractions and wanted to know nature by looking at it: he wrote about the metamorphosis of plants, the bones of the skull and colours. He maintained that there is a common form in all plants, the <strong>primal plant</strong> (<em>Urpflanze</em>), and he believed he had seen it with his own eyes.</p>\n<p>In July 1794, on leaving a meeting of the Jena Society of Naturalists, Goethe explained the primal plant to Schiller, whom he had disliked until then. Schiller listened and replied with a very Kantian sentence: ‘That is not an experience, that is an idea.’ For Kant, an idea is something that reason thinks but that cannot be given in experience. Goethe was annoyed, but he answered with humour: he was delighted to have ideas without knowing it and even to see them with his own eyes. Goethe told the story in 1817, in a text entitled <em>Fortunate Event</em>, because from that argument a friendship was born that lasted until Schiller’s death in 1805. It is the heart of <strong>Weimar Classicism</strong>: the two wrote more than a thousand letters to each other, published journals and epigrams together and corrected each other’s works. They were looking for an art that, like that of the Greeks, would unite feeling and form, nature and freedom, and leave behind the excesses of Sturm und Drang.</p>\n<div class=\"figwrap\"><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/monumento_goethe_schiller.jpg\" alt=\"Goethe–Schiller Monument in front of the German National Theatre in Weimar (Ernst Rietschel, 1857), in a photochrom from around 1900. The two poets hold a laurel wreath together\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Goethe–Schiller Monument in front of the German National Theatre in Weimar (Ernst Rietschel, 1857), in a photochrom from around 1900. The two poets hold a laurel wreath together</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/goethe_campagna.jpg\" alt=\"Goethe in the Roman Campagna (Johann Heinrich Wilhelm Tischbein, 1787), painted during the journey to Italy that led Goethe to classicism\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption><em>Goethe in the Roman Campagna</em> (Johann Heinrich Wilhelm Tischbein, 1787), painted during the journey to Italy that led Goethe to classicism</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/urpflanze_turpin.jpg\" alt=\"The primal plant according to Pierre Jean François Turpin, for a French edition of Goethe’s botanical writings (1837)\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The primal plant according to Pierre Jean François Turpin, for a French edition of Goethe’s botanical writings (1837)</figcaption></figure></div>\n<p>The argument of 1794 sums up the difference between the two. Goethe believes that the universal can be seen in the particular, if you look properly. Schiller, as a good Kantian, believes that the universal is supplied by reason. In the <em>Critique of Judgement</em>, Kant had spoken of an <strong>intuitive</strong> understanding, able to see the whole before the parts, and had said that we humans do not have one. Goethe answered him in a short essay, <em>Intuitive Judgement</em>: perhaps we do have it, if we dare to use it.</p>\n<p>That same distrust of theory is found in the work of his life, <strong><em>Faust</em></strong> (Part One, 1808). Faust is a scholar who has studied everything and knows nothing worth knowing. In a famous scene he tries to translate the opening of the Gospel of John, ‘In the beginning was the <em>Lógos</em>’. He tries ‘the Word’, then ‘the Mind’ (the Sense), then ‘the Power’, and finally he writes: ‘In the beginning was the <strong>Deed</strong>’. It is just after this decision that Mephistopheles appears, the devil with whom he will sign the pact.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: Faust swaps ‘word’ for ‘deed’. Is it a victory of life over theory or the first step towards the pact with the devil? What would Hamann, for whom reason is language, say?</p>\n\n<h3>Faust, Part Two: the developer and the evicted</h3>\n<p>Goethe worked on <em>Faust</em> for almost sixty years. The second part, finished shortly before his death, was published in 1832. In the last act, Faust is no longer the scholar shut away in his study: he is a powerful old man who has been granted a strip of coast by the emperor and is reclaiming it from the sea. Dykes, canals, a harbour, ships returning laden with riches. It is the project of his life, the ‘deed’ of the beginning carried through to the end: not to know the world, but to transform it and put it to work.</p>\n<p>In the middle of this new land there remains something that does not fit: the cottage of an old couple, <strong>Philemon and Baucis</strong>, with their lime trees and a little chapel. Goethe takes their names from Ovid’s <em>Metamorphoses</em>, where a poor couple take in two gods disguised as travellers and are rewarded. Here, too, they give shelter to a traveller, but nobody protects them. Faust has offered them a better estate if they will leave, and they do not want to leave their home. From his palace, the sound of the chapel bell spoils his triumph: that corner which is not his ruins the whole property for him. He orders Mephistopheles to move them out of the way. Mephistopheles and his three thugs settle the matter in their own way: they break down the door, the old couple die of fright, the traveller who tries to defend them is killed, and the cottage burns along with the lime trees. Faust curses what has happened — he wanted an exchange, not a robbery — but he keeps the land, and the work goes on.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Human victims had to bleed, at night cries of pain rang out; towards the sea ran rivers of fire, and in the morning there was a canal.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Baucis says this before the fire, describing how Faust’s works have been built. Mephistopheles, reporting what has happened, dismisses it with a biblical reference: what happens here is what has always happened, for Naboth’s vineyard already existed. In the Book of Kings, King Ahab covets the vineyard of his neighbour Naboth, who will not sell it, and Queen Jezebel has him killed so that it can be taken. The greed of the powerful for the poor man’s little plot of land is as old as the Bible; what is new is that it is now done in the name of progress and of a project that will benefit many.</p>\n<p>The ending is terribly ironic. Care strikes Faust blind. He hears spades and thinks it is his workers digging the last canal; in fact it is the lemures digging his grave. Then he dreams of one day seeing a free people on free land, and before that vision he speaks the words of the pact, ‘stay a while, you are so beautiful!’, and dies. The free land he dreams of is the land the fire cleared.</p>\n<p>This episode has been read as one of the first literary critiques of capitalist modernity. <strong>Marx</strong>, a great reader of Goethe, quotes <em>Faust</em> in <em>Capital</em> and uses Mephistopheles’ lines on money in the 1844 <em>Manuscripts</em>. <strong>Lukács</strong> saw in <em>Faust</em> the tragedy of humanity in the bourgeois age. And <strong>Marshall Berman</strong>, in <em>All That Is Solid Melts into Air</em> (1982), called Faust ‘the developer’: the first great developer in literature, who destroys not out of malice but because his project cannot allow anything to remain outside it. For Berman, Philemon and Baucis are the first victims of a logic that will be repeated in every modernisation: the old, the poor and whatever does not pay must disappear to make way for the new, and the violence is done by others on commission while the developer keeps his hands clean. Berman had lived it: he saw an expressway tear through his Bronx neighbourhood in the 1950s.</p>\n<p>On this reading, <em>Faust</em> portrays with brutal clarity the design of modern bourgeois society: the freedom and prosperity promised to all are built on the expulsion of those who will not or cannot sell. The exploration <a href=\"#teoria/hf-marx-biblioteca\">‘The library Marx wanted to publish’</a> and topic 21 make it possible to follow this thread through philosophy.</p>\n<div class=\"figwrap\"><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/filemon_baucis_rembrandt.jpg\" alt=\"Philemon and Baucis receive Jupiter and Mercury (Rembrandt, 1658). In Ovid, the hospitable couple are rewarded by the gods; in Goethe’s Faust, they are evicted and die when their house is burned\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption><em>Philemon and Baucis</em> receive Jupiter and Mercury (Rembrandt, 1658). In Ovid, the hospitable couple are rewarded by the gods; in Goethe’s <em>Faust</em>, they are evicted and die when their house is burned</figcaption></figure></div>\n<p>This suspicion of the bourgeois world is not Goethe's alone: the exploration <a href=\"#teoria/hf-corazon-piedra\">‘The heart of stone’</a> follows it through other novels, tales, poems and paintings of the period, from Goya to Dickens and from Balzac to Büchner.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: Faust says he wanted an exchange, not a robbery. Is someone innocent if they commission a project and do not want to know how it gets done? Think of evictions, expropriations or ‘regenerated’ neighbourhoods you know: who would Faust be today, who Mephistopheles, and who Philemon and Baucis?</p>\n<h2>6. Jena: early Romanticism and idealism (c. 1794-1800)</h2>\n<p>In 1794, Fichte arrives in Jena, with the backing of Goethe, who was a minister in Weimar. Fichte presents his philosophy as the culmination of Kant's: if the subject lays down the conditions of experience, perhaps everything starts from an <strong>I</strong> that posits itself and posits the world over against itself. The young people who listen to him are dazzled. Novalis fills hundreds of pages of <em>Fichte Studies</em> (1795-1796). In 1799, Fichte loses his chair, accused of atheism.</p>\n<p>Around him the first Romantic group formed: the brothers August Wilhelm and Friedrich Schlegel, Caroline Schlegel, Dorothea Veit, Ludwig Tieck, Novalis and Schelling, who taught at Jena from 1798. Their journal, <strong>Athenaeum</strong> (1798-1800), published short, provocative fragments instead of treatises. The most famous, fragment 116, defines Romanticism as a ‘progressive universal poetry’: a poetry that mixes all genres, unites poetry and philosophy, and is never finished, because its essence is to be always in the process of becoming. Novalis sums it up in another formula: ‘The world must be romanticised’, that is, we must give back to the ordinary its mystery.</p>\n<div class=\"figwrap\"><figure class=\"fig\"><img class=\"figimg\" src=\"media/galeria_museo/weimar/libro_athenaeum_1798.jpg\" alt=\"Title page of the first issue of Athenaeum (Berlin, 1798), the Schlegel brothers’ journal\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Title page of the first issue of <em>Athenaeum</em> (Berlin, 1798), the Schlegel brothers’ journal</figcaption></figure></div>\n<p>Meanwhile, at the Protestant seminary in Tübingen, three roommates, <strong>Hegel</strong>, <strong>Schelling</strong> and <strong>Hölderlin</strong>, had celebrated the French Revolution and read Kant, Spinoza and Plato together. Around 1796-1797 a short untitled text was written, which we know in Hegel’s handwriting and which is usually attributed to any one of the three: the <strong>Oldest System-Programme of German Idealism</strong>. It says that the highest act of reason is an <strong>aesthetic act</strong>, that truth and goodness are only united as sisters in beauty, and that the philosopher needs as much aesthetic power as the poet. It calls for a ‘mythology of reason’: philosophical ideas that become images so that the people can understand them. It is the Enlightenment, the aesthetics of Kant and Schiller and Romantic ambition on a single page.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Weimar Classicism (Goethe, Schiller)</th><th>Early Romanticism (Schlegel, Novalis)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Model</td><td>Greece: harmony, measure, finished form</td><td>The Middle Ages, Christianity, folk culture; the open work</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Underlying philosophy</td><td>Kant, as read by Schiller</td><td>Fichte, then Schelling</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Preferred form</td><td>Tragedy, the novel of formation, the classical poem</td><td>The fragment, the novel that mixes genres, irony</td></tr>\n<tr><td>The ideal</td><td>Totality achieved: the whole human being</td><td>Totality longed for: the infinite that is never reached</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Art and philosophy</td><td>They help each other, but they are different</td><td>They must merge: ‘poetry and philosophy should be united’ (Schlegel)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: the Romantics write fragments instead of treatises. Can you think seriously in fragments? What is most like a fragment from the <em>Athenaeum</em> today: an aphorism, a tweet, a song?</p>\n\n<h2>7. Timeline: literature and philosophy side by side</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Literature</th><th>Philosophy</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>1759</td><td></td><td>Hamann, <em>Socratic Memorabilia</em></td></tr>\n<tr><td>1770-1771</td><td>Goethe and Herder in Strasbourg</td><td>Kant, inaugural dissertation in Königsberg</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1772</td><td></td><td>Herder, <em>Treatise on the Origin of Language</em></td></tr>\n<tr><td>1774</td><td>Goethe, <em>Werther</em> and <em>Prometheus</em></td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>1781</td><td>Schiller, <em>The Robbers</em></td><td>Kant, <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em></td></tr>\n<tr><td>1784</td><td></td><td>Kant, <em>What Is Enlightenment?</em>; Hamann, <em>Metacritique</em> (unpublished) and letter to Kraus; Herder, <em>Ideas</em> (Part 1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1785</td><td>Schiller, <em>Ode to Joy</em></td><td>Kant, <em>Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals</em> and review of Herder; pantheism controversy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1786-1788</td><td>Goethe’s journey to Italy: classical turn</td><td>Kant, <em>Critique of Practical Reason</em> (1788)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1789</td><td>Schiller, professor of History at Jena</td><td>French Revolution</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1790</td><td>Goethe, <em>The Metamorphosis of Plants</em></td><td>Kant, <em>Critique of Judgement</em></td></tr>\n<tr><td>1793-1795</td><td>Schiller, <em>On Grace and Dignity</em> and <em>Letters on the Aesthetic Education</em></td><td>Kant, <em>Religion...</em> (2nd ed. with the note on Schiller, 1794); Fichte arrives in Jena (1794)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1794</td><td>The friendship between Goethe and Schiller begins</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>1795-1796</td><td>Goethe, <em>Wilhelm Meister’s Apprenticeship</em>; Schiller, <em>On Naïve and Sentimental Poetry</em>; <em>Xenien</em></td><td>Novalis, <em>Fichte Studies</em></td></tr>\n<tr><td>c. 1797</td><td>Hölderlin, <em>Hyperion</em> (1797-1799)</td><td><em>Oldest System-Programme of German Idealism</em></td></tr>\n<tr><td>1798-1800</td><td><em>Athenaeum</em> journal; Novalis, <em>Hymns to the Night</em> (1800)</td><td>Schelling at Jena; Fichte loses his chair (1799); Herder, <em>Metacritique</em> (1799) and <em>Kalligone</em> (1800)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1804-1805</td><td>Schiller dies (1805)</td><td>Kant dies (1804)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1807-1808</td><td>Goethe, <em>Faust I</em> (1808)</td><td>Hegel, <em>Phenomenology of Spirit</em> (1807)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n<h2>8. Texts with commentary</h2>\n<p>Our own translation from the German originals. Read them with the question of this exploration in mind: what does each text owe to the conversation between poets and philosophers?</p>\n\n<h3>Text 1. The Enlightenment and its reply (Kant, 1784; Hamann, 1784)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Enlightenment is the human being’s emergence from his self-incurred immaturity. Immaturity is the inability to make use of one’s own understanding without the guidance of another. [...] <em>Sapere aude</em>! Have courage to make use of your own understanding!</p>\n<p>True enlightenment consists in the emergence of the immature human being from a tutelage for which the Most High in person is to blame.</p></blockquote>\n<p>The first paragraph is the opening of Kant’s <em>What Is Enlightenment?</em>, which you already know from Topic 18. The second is Hamann’s parody in his letter to Christian Jacob Kraus (December 1784). Hamann keeps Kant’s words and turns them upside down with a pun: in German, <em>allerhöchst</em> (‘most high’) was the form of address used for the king. The problem is not only the immature person who does not dare to think, but the <strong>guardian</strong> who keeps him in that situation. Who is that guardian? The King of Prussia, Frederick II, whom Kant praises in his text, and also the Enlightenment philosophers themselves, who dictate to others how they should think. Enlightenment reason can also be a form of tutelage.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: who is more to blame when someone does not think for themselves, that person or whoever takes advantage of their not doing so? Think of a present-day example.</p>\n\n<h3>Text 2. Returning to oneself (Goethe, <em>Werther</em>, 1774)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>A wonderful serenity has taken possession of my entire soul, like these sweet spring mornings which I enjoy with my whole heart. (Letter of 10 May.)</p>\n<p>I return into myself and find a world. (Letter of 22 May.)</p></blockquote>\n<p>Werther knows the world with his heart. Nature is not an object to be measured, but something that is felt and that reflects the mood of whoever looks at it: the spring of the first letters turns into storm and winter when love becomes impossible. The second sentence is the key to Sturm und Drang: the world is inside the subject. Compared with Kant, it is a reversal. For Kant, the subject also contributes something to what it knows, but what it contributes is universal, the same in everyone (the forms of sensibility and the categories). For Werther, what the subject contributes is its feeling, unique and unrepeatable.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: ‘I return into myself and find a world.’ Is it a form of knowledge or a form of isolation? How does Werther end?</p>\n\n<h3>Text 3. The rebel (Goethe, <em>Prometheus</em>, 1774)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Here I sit, forming men / in my own image, / a race that shall be like me, / to suffer, to weep, / to enjoy and to rejoice, / and to pay no heed to you, / as I do.</p></blockquote>\n<p>This is the last stanza of the poem. Prometheus, the Titan who stole fire from the gods, speaks to Zeus with contempt: the gods have not helped him at all; everything has been done by his ‘glowing heart’. Now he himself creates free human beings. It is the genius of Sturm und Drang in its purest form: the creator who recognises no authority above himself. And it is also the poem which, read by Lessing, triggered the pantheism controversy.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: Kant speaks of autonomy (giving oneself the law); Prometheus, of rebellion. Are they the same thing? What does Prometheus lack to be autonomous in the Kantian sense?</p>\n\n<h3>Text 4. The good will and its caricature (Kant, 1785; Schiller, 1796)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>It is impossible to think of anything at all in the world, or indeed even beyond it, that could be considered good without limitation except a good will.</p></blockquote>\n<p>This is the first sentence of the <em>Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals</em>. Read it alongside Schiller’s two epigrams in section 4 (‘Gladly I serve my friends...’). Schiller does not deny that the good will is the only thing good without limitation. What he caricatures is the idea that an action loses its worth if we also enjoy doing it. Is the caricature fair? Kant never said that we should despise our friends; he said that moral worth does not lie in inclination. But the caricature points to a real weakness: if enjoyment does not count, what place is left for love and friendship in moral life?</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: reconstruct the answer Kant might give to the epigrams. Then decide who wins the argument and why.</p>\n\n<h3>Text 5. The human being who plays (Schiller, <em>Letters on the Aesthetic Education</em>, Letter 15, 1795)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>For, to put it plainly once and for all, man only plays when he is in the fullest sense of the word a human being, and he is only fully a human being when he plays.</p></blockquote>\n<p>‘Playing’ here does not mean passing the time. Play is the activity in which freedom and necessity coincide: the person who plays follows rules, but because they want to, and their activity serves no purpose outside itself. Schiller takes up the ‘free play’ of imagination and understanding with which Kant explained beauty and turns it into an ideal of life. The complete human being is neither the one who represses their feelings with reason (Kant’s risk) nor the one who lets themselves be carried away by them (Werther’s risk), but the one who has brought them into harmony.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: in which of your activities do you follow rules because you want to and not because you are made to? Do you feel ‘fully human’ in it, as Schiller says?</p>\n\n<h3>Text 6. ‘In the beginning was the Deed’ (Goethe, <em>Faust I</em>, 1808)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>It is written: ‘In the beginning was the Word.’ / Here I am stuck already. Who will help me on? / I cannot value the word so highly; / I must translate it some other way. [...] / The spirit helps me! Suddenly I see clearly / and confidently write: ‘In the beginning was the Deed.’</p></blockquote>\n<p>Faust is translating the <em>Lógos</em> of the Gospel of John. The Greek word brings together everything that philosophy has sought since the Presocratics (Topic 4): word, reason, meaning, law. Faust discards them one by one, because none of these things seems to him enough to explain the origin of everything, and he settles on the deed. It can be read as the manifesto of an age which, after Kant, no longer seeks only to know the world, but to create it: Fichte will make the I an ‘act’ that posits itself. But Goethe is being ironic: at that very moment Mephistopheles enters the scene.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: in what order would you put Faust’s four translations (word, meaning, power, deed)? Which of them stands ‘in the beginning’ of your own decisions?</p>\n\n<h3>Text 7. The poetry that never ends (Friedrich Schlegel, <em>Athenaeum</em>, fragment 116, 1798)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>Romantic poetry is a progressive universal poetry. [...] The Romantic kind of poetry is still in the process of becoming; indeed, that is its real essence: that it should forever be becoming and never be completed.</p></blockquote>\n<p>‘Universal’ because it wants to bring together all genres (poetry and prose, criticism and creation) and to unite poetry with philosophy and with life. ‘Progressive’ because it has no finished model to imitate, as the classics had in Greece: it moves forward without end. The idea comes from Fichte, for whom the I is an infinite task, and it clashes with the classical ideal of Goethe and Schiller, the perfect, closed work. If you compare it with Montaigne (exploration to Topic 13), a kinship appears: both prefer the open work to the closed system.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: is it a flaw or a virtue for a work never to be finished? Think of a series, a saga or a video game that is ‘never finished’.</p>\n\n<h3>Text 8. Beauty as the highest act of reason (<em>Oldest System-Programme of German Idealism</em>, c. 1797)</h3>\n<blockquote class=\"src\"><p>I am convinced that the highest act of reason, the one in which it embraces all ideas, is an aesthetic act, and that truth and goodness are united as sisters only in beauty. The philosopher must possess as much aesthetic power as the poet.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Kant had separated three domains: knowledge (truth), morality (the good) and taste (beauty), and the third critique tried to build bridges between them. The young men of Tübingen take a step that Kant would never have taken: beauty is not a bridge, but the summit. The text ends by calling for a ‘mythology of reason’. If philosophy does not become image and poetry, the people will not understand it, and if poetry does not become rational, the philosopher will be ashamed of it. It is the point of arrival of the conversation that began with Hamann and Herder: poets and philosophers no longer want to be two separate trades.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: go back to the question at the beginning. Can a novel or a poem change a philosophical idea? Use at least two examples from this exploration in your answer.</p>\n\n<h2>9. Dictionary</h2>\n<table class=\"glos\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Sturm und Drang</td><td>‘Storm and stress’. German literary movement (c. 1770-1785) that champions feeling, nature, individual freedom and genius against the rules of Enlightenment taste.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Weimar Classicism</td><td>The period of Goethe and Schiller (c. 1786-1805, above all from 1794) which, following the Greek model, seeks an art of harmony between feeling and form.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Early Romanticism</td><td>The Jena group (c. 1795-1800): the Schlegel brothers, Novalis, Tieck. Open work, fragment, irony, union of poetry and philosophy, longing for the infinite.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Genius</td><td>For Sturm und Drang, the creator who invents his own rules. For Kant, ‘the talent that gives the rule to art’, disciplined by taste.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Metacritique</td><td>A critique of the critique. Hamann and Herder gave this title to their replies to the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em>: Kant criticises reason, but does not examine the language in which that reason thinks.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pantheism controversy</td><td>A debate in 1785 between Jacobi, Mendelssohn and others about whether Lessing was a Spinozist and whether reason leads to Spinozism (God identified with nature).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Aesthetic disinterestedness</td><td>For Kant, pleasure in the beautiful does not depend on the desire to possess or use the object.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sublime</td><td>What overwhelms us by its size or its force (a stormy sea, a mountain) and at the same time makes us feel the superiority of our reason.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Beautiful soul</td><td>Schiller’s ideal: the person in whom duty and inclination coincide, because they have educated their feelings.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Grace and dignity</td><td>For Schiller, grace expresses the harmony between reason and sensibility; dignity, the victory of reason over sensibility when there is no harmony.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Play drive</td><td>In Schiller’s <em>Letters</em>, the drive that reconciles the sensuous drive and the formal drive. Its object is beauty.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Primal plant (<em>Urpflanze</em>)</td><td>A form common to all plants that Goethe believed he could see in nature. Schiller replied that it was an idea, not an experience.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Progressive universal poetry</td><td>Friedrich Schlegel’s definition of Romanticism: a poetry that brings together all genres, unites poetry, philosophy and life, and is never finished.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><em>Weltliteratur</em></td><td>‘World literature’. An expression Goethe popularised in 1827: the age of national literatures is over and the age of exchange between all of them is beginning.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>"}});
